A stream may contain heterogeneous demos, including pairs where the output is identity. Record each pair exactly, but avoid assuming the most recent example supplies the test's rule or that all examples instantiate one universal operation. Independently inspect the test's own geometry for simple relations, such as consecutive same-color markers in a row or column defining a segment to fill. Treat such patterns as hypotheses, not facts: replay against structurally relevant demos and preserve endpoints and unrelated cells unless the examples establish otherwise.

Request additional demos only when a specific unresolved distinction could change the predicted test output. Heterogeneity or uncertainty alone is not a discriminator. After correctness feedback, immediately finish the instance rather than continuing exploration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/mixed-demo-structure-transfer.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md", "content": "# Periodic-pattern transfer from sparse marker grids

A sparse input may encode a palette/order and phase for a periodic output, rather than a local fill operation. Read the output demo as a 2D sequence: identify the minimal period, row-to-row phase shift, and top-left phase. Verify exact candidate mappings cellwise; common alternatives include `(r+c) mod p` and `(c-r) mod p`, which may look alike but diverge on asymmetric patterns.

Do not transfer a periodic sequence from one compelling demo unless the same role-level rule reproduces every example. No-op pairs and outputs with apparently different palettes/periods are evidence that the operation may be conditional or that the current abstraction is wrong. Following an incorrect submission, binary feedback cannot pinpoint phase versus palette errors. Avoid arbitrary variant cycling; request a discriminating demo while available, and if capped, make one best-supported choice rather than repeat rejected outputs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-evidence-and-retry", "file": "references/identity-demo-interpretation.md", "content": "# Interpreting identity demonstrations

An input/output-identical demonstration is evidence, not a blank example. Record the complete identity result and ask what condition might distinguish it from transformed examples: object configuration, palette roles, dimensions, count, or spatial relation. Do not infer a universal motif-copying or expansion rule from one transformed example if another demo preserves its salient pattern. First test whether a coherent structural trigger explains both. If competing triggers predict different outputs for the test, request a further pair only when it can distinguish them; otherwise avoid a chain of requests that does not improve the test prediction. Replay every full pair under the proposed conditional rule before submitting.

In sparse-marker tasks, distinguish the marker field from a compact multicolor seed/object. A visible seed does not establish that every scattered marker instantiates that motif; test exact change masks and conditions before expansion.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-evidence-and-retry/references/identity-demo-interpretation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/case-study-cross-motif-inference.md", "content": "# Cross-motif inference lesson

A recurring failure mode is guessing a motif's footprint or anchor from an incomplete visual impression. Before submitting, extract the exact colored motif from the input, list its non-background cells as offsets from a chosen anchor, and infer how the anchor corresponds to each scattered marker. Then apply the proposed rule to all markers in the demo and reproduce every output cell, including preserved cells and clipping at boundaries. Use demos to resolve orientation/anchor ambiguity rather than repeatedly submitting speculative grids. Distinct instances may share an abstract operation while using different motifs, so transfer the operation but relearn each motif's geometry and anchor from its own examples.

Also audit output dimensions and row lengths mechanically when possible; a syntactically plausible grid may fail the ARC grid schema if any row differs in length.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/case-study-cross-motif-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-generalization.md", "content": "# Cross-demo generalization under diverse examples

Continual-ARC demos can vary sharply in grid size, palette, and the seed's shape or location while still instantiating an abstract common operation. Do not seek one literal template that can be copied unchanged from the first example. For each pair, record (1) background and marker roles, (2) exact seed footprint including background-valued cells, (3) output additions and any removals, (4) placement/alignment rule, and (5) whether unrelated cells are preserved. Compare these role-level summaries across every pair.

Validate competing hypotheses by predicting the complete output for each demo. Similar-looking seed colors are not proof that every marker receives a stamp; check changed-cell masks and unchanged regions. If no single rule explains all examples, request another demo while budget remains rather than submitting a candidate that only resembles one pair. Once the rule is supported, apply it mechanically to the test input and audit the serialized grid separately.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-generalization.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md", "content": "# Selecting demos for heterogeneous ARC examples

When available demonstrations differ sharply in grid size, palette, and object structure, log each pair's role colors, exact changed-cell mask, untouched/identity regions, and any plausible structural trigger. Treat a rule as supported only if it reproduces complete outputs, not merely the salient changed motif.

Before requesting another pair, name the competing hypotheses and the concrete observation that would distinguish them; ask only if that distinction can materially change the test prediction. Repeated requests without a targeted discriminator have cost but may not improve inference. In the same way, do not progress from one vivid example directly to a large speculative output: preserve the test input unless evidence supports a transformation. Keep this evidence-gathering discipline compatible with strict interfaces: each puzzle response still emits exactly one action JSON.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/post-rejection-action-gate.md", "content": "# Gate the next action after rejection

A rejected submission provides only a binary signal; it does not identify which cells were wrong. Do not respond with a speculative near-variant based on the same demos. Revisit the complete model: output shape, transformation trigger, role/color mapping, changed-cell mask, and unchanged regions. If multiple materially different hypotheses remain and the demo budget permits, request a demo chosen to separate them. Submit again only when there is a specific evidence-based model revision that reproduces all demonstrations.

In this instance, two visibly different demo pairs showed localized changes: one recolored only selected 1-cells to 2 while other 8-cells remained, and another filled much of the region bounded by 8 while preserving boundary cells. A test guess that globally filled the region around the 3-cells was rejected. The demos did not validate that broad fill operation on the test; after rejection, the sound next move is targeted evidence gathering rather than arbitrary color or footprint changes.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/post-rejection-action-gate.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md", "content": "# Attempt-cap handling and retry gate

In strict one-action puzzle interfaces, the protocol remains binding after the puzzle ends. When feedback says the instance is over—especially after exhausting the attempt cap—respond with exactly `{"action":"finish"}`. An empty assistant response or another `submit` is not a valid terminal action.

After a wrong submission, binary feedback gives no location information. Do not respond with a cosmetic or arbitrary variant (for example, changing only a suspected region's color). State the proposed model correction internally and test it against the complete demonstrations, including unchanged pairs and preserved cells. Only submit when the revised rule has evidence and reproduces all examples. If ambiguity remains and a demo is available, request one only to resolve a specific competing hypothesis; once demos are exhausted, further unsupported guesses compound cost and risk consuming the cap.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md", "content": "# Cross-demo changed-mask audit

When demos appear visually unrelated (different dimensions, background colors, seed motifs, and output colors), first compare their exact input/output differences rather than assuming a fixed motif-copy rule. For every pair, record: (1) role-level colors (background, seed, painted color), (2) exact changed-cell coordinates and their bounding boxes, (3) cells left unchanged inside apparent motifs, (4) any connected components or repeated offsets involved, and (5) a concrete rule that predicts both changed and unchanged cells.

A plausible hypothesis is not validated by resemblance. Replay it across *all* demos and require exact agreement, including untouched noise and cells that retain the background. In this session, several demos had completely different grid sizes and seed shapes, and a guessed connected-zero flood-fill failed; that illustrates why a visual/connectedness guess must be checked against demo outputs before submission. After a failed answer, re-evaluate the rule itself and seek a demo that distinguishes explicit alternatives, rather than making another unsupported guess. Keep each demo request tied to a named uncertainty, since requests and wrong submissions both incur cost.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/mixed-demo-consistency.md", "content": "# Mixed and identity demonstrations

A task's demos are evidence to reconcile, not examples to cherry-pick. Before inferring a rule, compare each pair's dimensions, palette, geometry, and exact input/output difference. If a new pair has different dimensions/palette or is an identity mapping while earlier pairs showed changes, do not force a single transformation from superficial commonalities. Consider conditional task variants or an unresolved inconsistency; request a further demo only if it can distinguish hypotheses. Validate every proposed rule against every pair before submission. An incorrect submission should trigger re-analysis of the rule and the full evidence set rather than another unvalidated guess.
